{"id":"RC-024","claim":"Doro 8035: first boot asks 'Have you used a smartphone before?' and offers a beginner mode with three large bottom buttons Call/View/Send; a hardware panic button on the back texts GPS coordinates to emergency contacts via 3-second long-press or triple-press.","q":["Q1"],"evidence_type":"direct","source_url":"https://www.expertreviews.co.uk/technology/phones/doro-8035-review","source_class":"L3","verbatim_quote":"can be set to a three-second long press, or three consecutive presses before springing into action","hedged":False,"notes":"Hardware SOS pattern; app equivalent = on-screen red button with cancel countdown (BIG Launcher)."},
{"id":"RC-025","claim":"Even Doro's dedicated senior phone lets Android leak through: permission popups appear on first use of features, the notification bar is unchanged, and jargon like 'haptic feedback' slips into settings.","q":["Q1"],"evidence_type":"direct","source_url":"https://www.expertreviews.co.uk/technology/phones/doro-8035-review","source_class":"L3","verbatim_quote":"Android can't help but poke out behind the scenes... The notifications bar, for example, is completely unchanged... you can set \"the haptic feedback\", which isn't a helpful term.","hedged":False,"notes":"Cross-product evidence: hiding stock-OS surfaces is the hardest, most-valued part of senior UX."},
